"\n\n\n\n\n\n## Ordered\n\n\n\n## Review\n\nThis was our very first meal after driving hard since dawn for our trip to Geoje.  \nWhen looking up good restaurants in Geoje, locals said there weren't many great food spots around, but we usually enjoy eating just about anything, so we didn't worry too much.  \nWhile pondering where to eat for our first meal, we settled on ramen, which felt like a relatively light option.  \nLooking at restaurant reviews, it had high ratings and usually drew quite a line, so we figured a Friday lunch would be less crowded than the weekend and decided to check it out.  \n\nThe restaurant was located in a villa building, and parking was available right at the building.  \nI was worried parking might be difficult since it's a residential villa building, but to my surprise, there was enough space to accommodate around eight cars.  \nMoreover, there were strangely almost no cars belonging to the residents, so we were able to park easily without any issues.  \nArriving early before opening by moving quickly also seemed to help.  \n\nEven though we arrived 20 minutes before opening, the doors were already open, and we could place our order right away via the kiosk.  \nIt was emphasized here that even when waiting in line, you must place your order first before waiting, and before opening was no exception.  \nWe ordered the Zuburasoba and the Original Tonkotsu.  \n\nAfter placing the order and completing the payment at the kiosk, an order slip comes out; you write down your contact phone number on it and drop it into the black bin next to the kiosk.  \nSince it was still before opening hours, we spent some time taking a light stroll around the neighborhood after ordering.  \nRight on opening time, they called the phone number we had written down, and we were finally able to enter the restaurant.  \n\nThe interior was relatively small with only a few tables, which gave it an authentic, cozy feel reminiscent of a local eatery in Japan.  \nInside the shop, an old Japanese documentary-style variety show was playing continuously.  \nIt followed an ordinary office worker struggling while working at a ramen shop to open a franchise; I watched closely wondering if it had any connection to this restaurant, but couldn't find any particular link.  \n\nAfter sitting down and waiting for a short while, the dishes we ordered came out promptly.  \n\nThe Zuburasoba was a soupless style, reminiscent of fried soba where the sauce coats the noodles thickly.  \nThe mala aroma came through very subtly without being too spicy, making it comfortable and enjoyable to eat.  \n\nThe Tonkotsu Ramen had a very rich broth, and the noodles were on the thinner side.  \nTo be honest, compared to the ramen I frequently enjoy in Seoul, it was certainly delicious, but I didn't feel it was exceptionally superior, which left me slightly disappointed.  \nStill, tasting the rich broth brewed with care allowed me to appreciate the owner's sincerity and hard work.  \n\nIt seems like a place worth stopping by at least once when visiting Geoje.  \nHowever, since the weekend wait times are said to be huge, you might want to plan carefully, and personally, I don't think it's worth waiting in line for 1 to 2 hours.\n## Pictures\n\n\n\n\n\n\n"